<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Religious legitimacy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Religious legitimacy is a recurring source of imperial strength, as rulers claim divine sanction or a sacred role to justify their authority and command loyalty. It can take the form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>, a caliphal title or a ruler's link to sacred institutions. Examples include the caliphal authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, the Zoroastrian kingship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, the papal coronation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba Empire</a>.</p></div>
